1=Stability test summary:
2=1. CTH23 sudden collapse @ 26 FC/DH 1.5-2mm above 0310 CR.
3=2. STH @ 16 DH 3.0mm, levered column
4=
5=Snowpack generally unconsolidated to Oct rain crust (0310 CR). Surface Hoar buried on surface by a skiff of snow.
6=
7=2 WEEK SUMMARY: We have been in a holding pattern for some time in terms of the Danger rating. Last significant
8=snow was Jan 15/16 8 cm preceeded by 22cm on Dec 18. Current HS is 94. Over the terrain, snow can range from 0 to
9=2m.
10=Slope tests: generally in the Moderate to Hard range except where Jan 1st's surface can be found buried in protected
11=locations. Recent unseasonably warm temperatures have 'tightened' the snowpack and strong winds in alpine created a
12=hard and thick wind slab.
13=Structure: BTL-unconsolidated to ground, TL-generally unconsolidated until breaking into alpine where wind action has
14=created a firm windslab. ALP-hard thick supportive windslab.
15=Snow temperatures: recent unseasonable warm temperatures was supporting rounding process in upper layers of
16=snowpack.
17=
18=WEATHER 2 WEEK SUMMARY: Generally warm temperatures with little new snow. Jan 15/16th 8 cm was only storm
19=during this period.
20=Penetration: Ram penetration has hovered around 20-30cm. BTL unsupportive, ALP-supportive on hard wind slab.
21=Winds: Strong Westerly winds for 6 days Jan 10-15.
22=Temps: +2 C maximum for a few days with general average hovering around 0 to -1 C. Lows -10. Feels like spring.
23=Settlement: Warm temperatures sped up the settlement process as we are typically in a deep freeze at this time of year.
24=Freezing level: Warm days BTL caused water seeping out of rocks, wet snow, and a few wet point release ava.. Many
25=alpine sun affected slopes were warming up substantially as well.
26=
27=AVALANCHE OBSERVATIONS 2 WEEK SUMMARY: A few sz 1 slab ava in the alpine after the 8 cm of new snow Jan
28=16th. This was shortly followed by sz 1.5's loose, moist slides at all elevations but more so BTL with the warm
29=temperatures.
30=
31=STABILITY 2 WEEK SUMMARY: The stability has been improving over the last 2 weeks as warm temperatures settle the
32=snowpack and thick windslabs in alpine appear to be bridging well.
